Z||000000||Mach10||07-15-2001||03:36 PM||||Just before I took my car apart to do some general maintenance/repairs, I had taken it on a straight road to sort out some bearing noise. In a straight line, I get a slight whirring, the kind I associate with wheel bearings. After swinging the car back and forth, I decided that it was the left front. Now, after replacing, and repacking, I turned the stereo down after my ears picking up a slight noise. Sure enough, the noise is still there. AND IT STILL SOUNDS LIKE IT'S ON THE SAME SIDE. <BR>When I turn right, the sound gets louder, when I turn left, the almost disappears. When I break, it gets a little louder. Should I just go and change the other side's bearings, or am I barking up the wrong tree?||||reg||13
Z||000001||Raydar||07-15-2001||03:50 PM||||Try rotating your tires front to back. Tires that are worn unevenly can make all sorts of strange noise. I had a worn tire once, that had me absolutely convinced I had a bad bearing. Wrong-O. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mach10||07-15-2001||03:55 PM||||Now that you mention it, while I was polishing my wheels, i'd notice marks from wieghts, but I never saw any balancing weights, either on the inside, or out... HMMmmmmmm... I wonder...||||reg||1
